var markup_default = "<a class=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main\">Skip to study content</a>\n<header><div class=\"brand\"><div class=\"emblem\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><div><div class=\"brand-name\">Beyond the Conditioned</div><small>A STUDY OF DEPENDENT ARISING</small></div></div><nav class=\"header-actions\" aria-label=\"Presentation tools\"><button class=\"small\" id=\"sources\">Sources</button><a class=\"header-reading\" href=\"#further-reading\">Reading list ↓</a><button class=\"small\" id=\"theme-toggle\" type=\"button\" aria-pressed=\"false\" title=\"Paper is the default study palette. Switch to a night palette for low light.\">Night</button><button class=\"small fullscreen\" id=\"fullscreen\">Full screen ↗</button></nav></header>\n<nav class=\"toc-progress\" id=\"toc-progress\" aria-label=\"Place in this page\">\n<p class=\"toc-copy\"><strong id=\"toc-index\">1 of 4</strong><span aria-hidden=\"true\"> · </span><span id=\"toc-name\">Explore the model</span></p>\n<ol class=\"toc-dots\">\n<li><a href=\"#explore\" data-toc=\"explore\" aria-current=\"true\">Explore</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#study-guide\" data-toc=\"study-guide\">Twelve links</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#study-deeper\" data-toc=\"study-deeper\">Deeper</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#further-reading\" data-toc=\"further-reading\">Books</a></li>\n</ol>\n</nav>\n<main class=\"workspace\" id=\"main\" tabindex=\"-1\">\n<div class=\"introline\"><div><div class=\"eyebrow\">Paṭiccasamuppāda · The twelve links</div><h1>Dependent arising.<br>The conditions of suffering and release.</h1></div><div class=\"segmented\" aria-label=\"View of dependent arising\"><button id=\"arising\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Arising</button><button id=\"cessation\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Cessation</button></div></div>\n<nav class=\"section-nav\" aria-label=\"Study sections\"><a href=\"#explore\">Explore the model</a><a href=\"#study-guide\">Read all twelve links</a><a href=\"#study-deeper\">Study deeper</a><a href=\"#further-reading\">Books & scholarship</a><button class=\"small\" id=\"formulations\">Compare formulations ↗</button><button class=\"small\" id=\"path\">The path toward release ↗</button></nav>\n<p class=\"audience\"><strong>For curious newcomers and dharma students.</strong> Explore independently or use the guide during a talk. No prior Pāli knowledge is needed. <button class=\"small\" id=\"glossary\">Key terms ↗</button></p><p class=\"scope-line\">The twelve links in SN 12.2, with selected Chinese Āgama parallels. Explore the texts, their interpretations, and the practice of understanding conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"cessation-banner\" id=\"cessation-banner\" hidden><strong>Cessation is not sensory shutdown.</strong> An awakened living person still experiences contact and feeling. This view illustrates the ending of the conditions sustaining suffering and renewed existence.</div>\n<div class=\"layout\" id=\"explore\">\n<section class=\"visual\" id=\"visual\" aria-label=\"Interactive open ring of twelve links\">\n<div class=\"scene\" id=\"scene\"><div class=\"scene-top\"><span id=\"view-label\">3D OPEN RING</span><span>12 LINKS · 11 CONNECTIONS</span></div><canvas id=\"canvas\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></canvas><div class=\"node-layer\" id=\"nodes\" aria-label=\"Select a link\"></div><div class=\"center-copy\"><div class=\"eyebrow\" id=\"center-mode\">Conditional arising</div><div class=\"center-title\" id=\"center-title\">With this condition,<br>that arises.</div><div class=\"center-sub\" id=\"center-sub\">A map of suffering<br>and the possibility of release.</div></div><div class=\"reading\" id=\"reading\" aria-label=\"Readable link overview\"></div></div>\n<div class=\"viewbar\"><div class=\"viewtools\"><button class=\"small\" id=\"symbols\">Traditional symbols</button><button class=\"small\" id=\"reading-toggle\" aria-pressed=\"false\">2D reading view</button><button class=\"small orbit-control\" id=\"rotate-left\" aria-label=\"Rotate left\">↶</button><button class=\"small orbit-control\" id=\"rotate-right\" aria-label=\"Rotate right\">↷</button><button class=\"small\" id=\"reset\">Reset view</button></div><span class=\"hint\" id=\"view-hint\">Drag to orbit · Select a numbered link</span></div>\n<div class=\"legend\" id=\"ring-legend\">Filled node: selected · Emphasized neighbors: preceding/following links · The ring remains open</div></section>\n<aside class=\"panel\" aria-label=\"Selected link explanation\" tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"detail-panel\"><div class=\"panel-inner\" id=\"panel\"></div></aside>\n</div>\n<p class=\"arrow-note\">Arrows show conditioning relationships stated in this discourse, not every condition involved. Neighbor highlighting is a reading aid. <a href=\"#study-arrows\" data-study=\"study-arrows\">What do the arrows mean?</a></p><div class=\"journeybar\"><div><div class=\"eyebrow\" id=\"progress-label\">Your guided journey starts here</div><div class=\"steps\" id=\"steps\" aria-label=\"Jump to a link\"></div></div><div class=\"journey-actions\"><button id=\"restart\" title=\"Return to the introduction\">Restart</button><button id=\"back\" disabled>← Back</button><button class=\"primary\" id=\"next\">Begin journey →</button></div></div>\n<div class=\"bottom\"><div>Early-discourse foundation · Educational visualization, not a mechanical simulation.<br>Arrows express conditions, not equal time intervals or sufficient causes.</div><div class=\"viewtools\"><button class=\"small\" id=\"example\">A daily-life example ↗</button><button class=\"small\" id=\"notes\">Presenter notes</button></div></div><div class=\"toast\" id=\"toast\" role=\"status\"></div><div class=\"sr-only\" id=\"announcement\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\n<section class=\"section-block\" id=\"study-guide\" aria-labelledby=\"guide-title\"><div class=\"eyebrow\">Read at your own pace</div><h2 id=\"guide-title\">The twelve links, in plain language.</h2><p class=\"section-lead\">This readable companion follows SN 12.2. Definitions are paraphrases; qualifications help distinguish the text from later interpretation. Contact and feeling also draw on MN 148.</p><p class=\"meta-scope\">The model’s arrows state conditions, not a single sufficient cause or a timetable. The teaching addresses suffering and renewed existence; it does not establish an unchanging self.</p><div class=\"guide-actions\"><button id=\"print-guide\">Print study guide</button><button id=\"compare-models\">How do interpretations differ?</button></div><div class=\"text-guide\"><article id=\"guide-01\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 01</span><h3>Ignorance</h3><div class=\"pali\">Avijjā</div><p>Not understanding suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path leading to its cessation.</p><p class=\"distinction\">Ignorance is the first item in this formula, not a first cause of the universe.</p><a href=\"#link-01-arising\" data-jump=\"0\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-02\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 02</span><h3>Volitional formations</h3><div class=\"pali\">Saṅkhārā</div><p>Intentional activities of body, speech, and mind, conditioned here by ignorance.</p><p class=\"distinction\">The word has different uses in Buddhist texts. Here it belongs to the dependent-arising formula.</p><a href=\"#link-02-arising\" data-jump=\"1\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-03\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 03</span><h3>Consciousness</h3><div class=\"pali\">Viññāṇa</div><p>Consciousness associated with seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting, bodily sensing, and mental objects.</p><p class=\"distinction\">Other early sequences describe consciousness and name-and-form as mutually conditioning. This diagram follows SN 12.2.</p><a href=\"#link-03-arising\" data-jump=\"2\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-04\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 04</span><h3>Name-and-form</h3><div class=\"pali\">Nāma-rūpa</div><p>Mental factors and physical form: the embodied complex involved in experience.</p><p class=\"distinction\">Here “name-and-form” refers to mental factors and material form, rather than an object’s label and appearance.</p><a href=\"#link-04-arising\" data-jump=\"3\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-05\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 05</span><h3>Six sense bases</h3><div class=\"pali\">Saḷāyatana</div><p>The eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind as the six bases of experience.</p><p class=\"distinction\">This classification is a Buddhist account of experience, not a claim about the modern anatomical count of senses.</p><a href=\"#link-05-arising\" data-jump=\"4\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-06\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 06</span><h3>Contact</h3><div class=\"pali\">Phassa</div><p>The meeting of a sense faculty, its object, and the corresponding consciousness.</p><p class=\"distinction\">Contact includes hearing and thinking as well as physical touching.</p><a href=\"#link-06-arising\" data-jump=\"5\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-07\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 07</span><h3>Feeling</h3><div class=\"pali\">Vedanā</div><p>The pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral tone of an experience.</p><p class=\"distinction\">Feeling tone is one aspect of experience; anger is a more complex emotion. An awakened person can feel without craving.</p><a href=\"#link-07-arising\" data-jump=\"6\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-08\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 08</span><h3>Craving</h3><div class=\"pali\">Taṇhā</div><p>Thirst for experiences through the six senses; elsewhere also classified as craving for sensuality, existence, and nonexistence.</p><p class=\"distinction\">Wholesome aspiration and intention are distinguished from craving.</p><a href=\"#link-08-arising\" data-jump=\"7\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-09\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 09</span><h3>Clinging</h3><div class=\"pali\">Upādāna</div><p>Grasping at sensual pleasures, views, rules and observances, or doctrines of self.</p><p class=\"distinction\">The concern includes treating practices as sufficient for liberation; ethical practice itself remains valuable.</p><a href=\"#link-09-arising\" data-jump=\"8\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-10\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 10</span><h3>Becoming</h3><div class=\"pali\">Bhava</div><p>Existence or becoming in the sensual, form, and formless domains, conditioning birth.</p><p class=\"distinction\">Reducing this link to adopting a temporary identity would leave out the early texts’ rebirth framework.</p><a href=\"#link-10-arising\" data-jump=\"9\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-11\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 11</span><h3>Birth</h3><div class=\"pali\">Jāti</div><p>The coming into existence of beings, including the appearance of the aggregates and acquisition of sense bases.</p><p class=\"distinction\">“Birth of an ego-state” offers an interpretive application. SN 12.2 has a broader account of birth.</p><a href=\"#link-11-arising\" data-jump=\"10\">Explore this link →</a></article><article id=\"guide-12\"><span class=\"guide-number\">LINK 12</span><h3>Aging-and-death</h3><div class=\"pali\">Jarāmaraṇa</div><p>Aging and dying, together with the sorrow, lamentation, pain, distress, and despair described in the formula.</p><p class=\"distinction\">There is no additional “death causes ignorance” arrow in the standard formula. The ring is intentionally open.</p><a href=\"#link-12-arising\" data-jump=\"11\">Explore this link →</a></article></div></section>\n<section class=\"section-block\" id=\"study-deeper\" aria-labelledby=\"deeper-title\"><div class=\"eyebrow\">Optional exploration · open one question at a time</div><h2 id=\"deeper-title\">Look a little deeper.</h2><p class=\"section-lead\">Start with the early-discourse foundations. Explore later philosophy and historical arguments when you want more context. Each note identifies its source and scope.</p><div class=\"study-layout\"><div class=\"study-group\"><h3>Understanding the teaching</h3>\n<details class=\"study-topic\" id=\"study-parallels\"><summary>How do the Pāli and Chinese texts compare?</summary><div class=\"study-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Compare textual versions</p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Selected parallel witnesses</div><p>The main ring follows SN 12.2. Related discourses preserved in Chinese let us examine both shared teachings and differences in transmission. A parallel is a related witness, not an identical text.</p><p>These are editorial English summaries of selected passages. SĀ numbers follow the standard discourse numbering of the Chinese <em>Saṃyukta Āgama</em>, Taishō no. 99 (T 99), rather than the alternative numbers shown in the linked editions. The Chinese texts are punctuated by Zhuang Chunjiang (莊春江); his accompanying SN translations are from Pāli into Chinese.</p>\n<details class=\"witness\"><summary>SN 12.2 / SĀ 298 · Definitions</summary><div class=\"witness-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Pāli and Chinese texts · Definitions</p><h4>Shared teaching</h4><p>Both present the twelve-link framework. They share the six classes of consciousness and the six internal sense bases.</p><h4>Different classifications</h4><dl class=\"truth-list\"><dt>Ignorance</dt><dd>SN 12.2 defines ignorance through not knowing the Four Noble Truths. SĀ 298 gives a longer list, including action and results.</dd><dt>Name</dt><dd>SN 12.2: feeling, perception, intention, contact, attention. SĀ 298: the four non-material aggregates, including consciousness.</dd><dt>Feeling</dt><dd>SN 12.2: six classes according to the contact from which feeling arises. SĀ 298: pleasant, painful, and neither-painful-nor-pleasant.</dd><dt>Craving</dt><dd>SN 12.2: six classes according to their objects. SĀ 298: craving associated with sensuality, form, and formlessness. This differs from the sensuality/existence/nonexistence classification cited elsewhere on this page.</dd></dl><h4>Why it matters</h4><p>The framework is shared, but definitions require a named source. Our plain-language feeling gloss uses feeling tone; the Text layer also records SN 12.2’s six contact-based classes.</p><a href=\"https://agama.buddhason.org/SA/SA0298.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Compare SN 12.2 / SĀ 298 · Chinese and Pāli ↗</a><p class=\"review-note\">Passage locator: SĀ 298, the explanations beginning 無明, 名, 受, and 愛; SN 12.2, the corresponding definitions. Select the text’s title carefully: the linked page also includes EĀ 49.5.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"witness\"><summary>SN 12.20 / SĀ 296 · Principle and phenomena</summary><div class=\"witness-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Pāli and Chinese texts · Principle and phenomena</p><h4>Shared teaching</h4><p>The texts distinguish conditionality from dependently arisen phenomena and describe the regularity discovered and taught by a Buddha.</p><h4>Different presentation</h4><p>SN 12.20 explicitly describes each conditioned link as impermanent and subject to cessation. SĀ 296 arranges its explanations of the principle and conditioned phenomena differently. The two passages should be read in their own order.</p><h4>Why it matters</h4><p>The twelve links are an application of conditionality. Anālayo’s discussion of SN 12.20 helps explain why shorter formulations also express dependent arising.</p><a href=\"https://agama.buddhason.org/SA/SA0296.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Compare SN 12.20 / SĀ 296 · Chinese and Pāli ↗</a><p class=\"review-note\">Passage locator: the opening distinction between 因緣法 and 緣生法 in SĀ 296; the opening definitions in SN 12.20. See also Anālayo, “Dependent Arising” (2020).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"witness\"><summary>SN 12.67 / SĀ 288 · Mutual support</summary><div class=\"witness-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Pāli and Chinese texts · Mutual support</p><h4>Shared teaching</h4><p>Both accounts describe reciprocal support between consciousness and name-and-form. This relationship is explicit in the texts.</p><h4>Different analogy</h4><p>SN 12.67 uses two bundles of reeds leaning together. SĀ 288 describes three reeds standing through mutual support. The Chinese analogy does not, by itself, establish a third doctrinal factor.</p><h4>Why it matters</h4><p>Reciprocity has support in both witnesses. The relation between this account and the standard sequence remains a matter of interpretation. We preserve the analogy’s difference without ranking either version as automatically more authentic.</p><a href=\"https://agama.buddhason.org/SA/SA0288.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Compare SN 12.67 / SĀ 288 · Chinese and Pāli ↗</a><p class=\"review-note\">Passage locator: SĀ 288, the analogy beginning 譬如：三蘆; SN 12.67, the passage beginning Seyyathāpi … dve naḷakalāpiyo.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"witness\"><summary>SN 12.15 / SĀ 301 · The middle teaching</summary><div class=\"witness-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Pāli and Chinese texts · The middle teaching</p><h4>Shared teaching</h4><p>Both connect dependent arising with a teaching that avoids the extremes of existence and nonexistence.</p><h4>Reading the witnesses</h4><p>The parallel supports the central theme. Its wording and presentation should be consulted separately rather than combined into a single reconstructed quotation.</p><h4>Why it matters</h4><p>Our Middle Way note has a comparative foundation. Agreement between witnesses strengthens a reading without establishing the Buddha’s exact original words.</p><a href=\"https://agama.buddhason.org/SA/SA0301.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Compare SN 12.15 / SĀ 301 · Chinese and Pāli ↗</a><p class=\"review-note\">Passage locator: the discussion of 有 and 無 followed by the middle teaching in SĀ 301; the corresponding existence/nonexistence passage in SN 12.15.</p></div></details>\n<p class=\"review-note\">These selected comparisons are a starting point, not a complete critical edition. Wording, translation, and historical interpretation are separate questions.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"study-topic\" id=\"study-arrows\"><summary>What do the arrows mean?</summary><div class=\"study-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · What the arrows mean</p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Scholarly explanation of early discourses</div><p>Read each arrow as “with this as a condition.” The diagram selects relationships for study; several conditions may be involved, and the spacing carries no temporal measure.</p><p>Bodhi explains that the discourses include mutual support and other forms of conditionality. His introduction discusses variants that connect the factors differently. The relationships are more complex than a row of independent objects knocking one another over.</p><p>The open ring preserves the sequence in SN 12.2. Neighboring highlights guide your reading while leaving other conditioning relationships outside this view.</p><p>Kalupahana’s philosophical account distinguishes conditionality from fatalism: the conditions matter. It should be read as an attributed interpretation, not as a resolution of every debate about causation or free will.</p><p class=\"review-note\">Bodhi, <em>Connected Discourses</em> (2000), vol. I, pp. 522–524; Kalupahana, <em>Causality</em> (1975), pp. 89–95. Compare <a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.067.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SN 12.67 ↗</a>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"study-topic\" id=\"study-middle\"><summary>Why is this called a middle teaching?</summary><div class=\"study-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · A middle teaching</p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Early discourse · SN 12.15</div><p>SN 12.15 presents dependent arising between the extremes “everything exists” and “everything does not exist.” It directs attention to arising and cessation rather than attachment to a position about an enduring self.</p><p>Here, “middle” names an understanding of experience through its conditions, rather than a compromise between two opinions.</p><p>Cessation concerns the ending of the conditions sustaining suffering, rather than the destruction of a permanent self.</p><p class=\"review-note\"><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.015.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read SN 12.15 ↗</a> · Bodhi, <em>Connected Discourses</em>, vol. I, pp. 544–545; introduction, pp. 522–524. This is an introductory paraphrase, not an exhaustive interpretation.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"study-topic\" id=\"study-self\"><summary>Does experience require a permanent self?</summary><div class=\"study-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Self and conditions</p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Early discourse · SN 12.12</div><p>In SN 12.12, questions such as “Who feels?” are redirected toward the conditions for feeling. The inquiry proceeds through conditions, without positing an unchanging owner behind the process.</p><div class=\"relation\"><div class=\"eyebrow\">A change in the question</div><p>From “Who is the permanent experiencer?” to “With what condition does feeling arise?”</p></div><p>The discourse identifies contact as the condition for feeling. Pain remains real, and conduct and care matter. The inquiry examines experience without assuming an enduring substance that owns it.</p><p class=\"review-note\"><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.012.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read SN 12.12 ↗</a> · Bodhi, <em>Connected Discourses</em>, vol. I, pp. 541–542, and introduction, p. 524. The question shift above is an explanatory paraphrase.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"study-topic\" id=\"study-truths\"><summary>How do the Four Noble Truths fit?</summary><div class=\"study-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Four Noble Truths</p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Early-discourse framework · scholarly explanation</div><p>Bodhi connects the arising and cessation sequences with the second and third noble truths. The wider framework explains the purpose of studying these conditions.</p><dl class=\"truth-list\"><dt>1 · Suffering</dt><dd>What is to be understood.</dd><dt>2 · Its origin</dt><dd>The sustaining conditions investigated in the arising sequence.</dd><dt>3 · Its cessation</dt><dd>The ending of suffering through the ending of its sustaining conditions.</dd><dt>4 · The path</dt><dd>The practice cultivated toward cessation: the Noble Eightfold Path.</dd></dl><p>The Upanisā sequence describes conditions supporting liberation. It complements the Eightfold Path while retaining its own name and distinct list of factors.</p><div class=\"study-actions\"><button class=\"small\" data-study-mode=\"arising\">Explore arising ↑</button><button class=\"small\" data-study-mode=\"cessation\">Explore cessation ↑</button><button class=\"small\" id=\"deeper-path\">Explore supporting conditions</button></div><p class=\"review-note\">Bodhi, <em>Connected Discourses</em>, vol. I, pp. 522–524; SN 12.43. For the Eightfold Path, <a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.065.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SN 12.65 ↗</a>; for the companion sequence, SN 12.23.</p></div></details>\n</div><div class=\"study-group\"><h3>Philosophy & historical questions</h3>\n<details class=\"study-topic\" id=\"study-emptiness\"><summary>How does this relate to emptiness?</summary><div class=\"study-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Emptiness</p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Madhyamaka · Nāgārjuna through Garfield</div><p>In <em>Mūlamadhyamakakārikā</em> 24.18–19, Nāgārjuna connects dependent arising, emptiness, and dependent designation. In Garfield’s interpretation, things exist conventionally and function through dependence, without an independent, intrinsic nature.</p><p>Dependence makes change and practice intelligible. If suffering possessed a fixed, independent nature, its cessation would be impossible. Emptiness names this absence of intrinsic nature, not a hidden substance or permanent foundation beneath appearances.</p><p>Chapter 26 returns explicitly to the twelve links and liberation. This is a later Madhyamaka philosophical treatment, not a substitute for the wording of SN 12.2.</p><p class=\"review-note\">Jay L. Garfield, <em>The Fundamental Wisdom of the Middle Way</em> (1995), Chapter 24, especially verses 18–19 and their commentary; Chapter 26. Garfield’s preface identifies his Indo-Tibetan Prāsaṅgika interpretive standpoint. Other readings exist.</p><a href=\"#book-garfield\">Find this book in the reading list ↓</a></div></details>\n<details class=\"study-topic\" id=\"study-history\"><summary>Was the formula a response to Vedic ideas?</summary><div class=\"study-body\"><p class=\"study-crumb\">Study deeper · Historical questions</p><div class=\"eyebrow\">Historical reconstruction · scholarly argument</div><p>Joanna Jurewicz proposes that the twelve-link formula reworks Vedic accounts of creation while rejecting their assumption of an underlying self. Her paper compares the sequence and its terminology with earlier Vedic material.</p><p>She offers a tentative reconstruction with a limited scope: some canonical formulations fall outside her treatment. Read it as a historical argument, distinct from an established definition or the Buddha’s own recorded explanation.</p><p>Richard Gombrich endorses her proposal in his second-edition introduction. This is an endorsement of her argument, rather than independent confirmation. His Chapter II also discusses conditioned consciousness and the teachings’ Brahmanical context.</p><h4>Read with two questions</h4><p>What evidence supports the historical connection? What remains an inference about the teaching’s original setting?</p><p class=\"review-note\">Jurewicz, “Playing with Fire,” <em>Journal of the Pali Text Society</em> 26 (2000), pp. 77–103. Gombrich, <em>How Buddhism Began</em>, 2nd edition, introduction and Chapter II.</p><a href=\"#book-jurewicz\">Find these readings below ↓</a></div></details>\n</div></div></section>\n<section class=\"section-block\" id=\"further-reading\" aria-labelledby=\"reading-title\"><div class=\"eyebrow\">A reading path, not a consensus claim</div><h2 id=\"reading-title\">Books & scholarship.</h2><p class=\"section-lead\">Start with an accessible account, return to the discourses, then compare interpretations. These selections include academic scholarship and traditional scholar-monks; their approaches are not interchangeable.</p><div class=\"reading-route\"><div><strong>01 · Get oriented</strong><p>Gethin or Harvey for a book-length introduction; Anālayo for a short conceptual entry.</p></div><div><strong>02 · Read the sources</strong><p>Begin with SN 12.2. Then compare SN 12.67 and SN 12.23 using the links below or Bodhi’s translations.</p></div><div><strong>03 · Deepen the inquiry</strong><p>Read Bodhi on liberation, then choose philosophy or historical debate. Keep each author’s interpretive standpoint in view.</p></div></div><div class=\"book-filter\" aria-label=\"Filter reading list\"><button data-filter=\"all\" aria-pressed=\"true\">All readings</button><button data-filter=\"intro\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Introductions</button><button data-filter=\"primary\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Primary-text books</button><button data-filter=\"commentary\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Commentary</button><button data-filter=\"research\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Articles</button><button data-filter=\"advanced\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Advanced studies</button></div><div class=\"reading-count\" id=\"reading-count\" aria-live=\"polite\">11 readings · 8 books, 1 booklet, 2 articles</div><div class=\"book-grid\"><article class=\"book\" data-category=\"intro\" data-print-url=\"https://global.oup.com/academic/product/the-foundations-of-buddhism-9780192892232\"><div class=\"tag\">Academic introduction</div><h3>The Foundations of Buddhism</h3><div class=\"bib\">Rupert Gethin<br>Oxford University Press, 1998.</div><p>Begin with Chapter 6, especially pp. 149–159. A clear comparison of the twelve links, classical three-life and momentary readings, and the Wheel of Life.</p><p class=\"note\">Best first book for seeing how the interpretations fit together.</p><div class=\"locate\">ISBN 9780192892232</div><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"https://global.oup.com/academic/product/the-foundations-of-buddhism-9780192892232\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Publisher · book ↗</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" data-category=\"intro\" data-print-url=\"https://www.cambridge.org/9780521859424\"><div class=\"tag\">Academic introduction</div><h3>An Introduction to Buddhism: Teachings, History and Practices</h3><div class=\"bib\">Peter Harvey<br>Cambridge University Press, 2nd edition, 2013.</div><p>Read “Conditioned Arising,” pp. 65–72. Explains specific conditions, rebirth, present experience, and why the ring should not add a death-to-ignorance arrow.</p><p class=\"note\">Best companion for terminology and interpretive cautions.</p><div class=\"locate\">ISBN 9780521676748 (paperback)</div><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/9780521859424\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Publisher · book ↗</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" data-category=\"primary\" data-print-url=\"https://wisdomexperience.org/product/buddhas-words/\"><div class=\"tag\">Translated primary texts</div><h3>In the Buddha’s Words: An Anthology of Discourses from the Pāli Canon</h3><div class=\"bib\">Bhikkhu Bodhi, editor and translator<br>Wisdom Publications, 2005.</div><p>A thematic route into the discourses with introductions. Read the material on wisdom and dependent origination alongside the broader account of the path.</p><p class=\"note\">An accessible next step from an overview to source texts.</p><div class=\"locate\">Anthology; scholarly introductions from a Theravāda perspective.</div><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"https://wisdomexperience.org/product/buddhas-words/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Publisher · book ↗</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" data-category=\"primary\" data-print-url=\"https://wisdomexperience.org/product/connected-discourses-buddha/\"><div class=\"tag\">Translated primary texts</div><h3>The Connected Discourses of the Buddha: A Translation of the Saṃyutta Nikāya</h3><div class=\"bib\">Bhikkhu Bodhi, translator<br>Wisdom Publications, 2000.</div><p>Study the Nidāna-saṃyutta (SN 12), with its introduction and notes. Compare different formulations rather than relying on one isolated passage.</p><p class=\"note\">For sustained source study; more demanding than an introductory book.</p><div class=\"locate\">ISBN 9780861713318</div><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"https://wisdomexperience.org/product/connected-discourses-buddha/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Publisher · book ↗</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" data-category=\"commentary\" data-print-url=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/bodhi/wheel277.html\"><div class=\"tag\">Traditional scholarly commentary · booklet</div><h3>Transcendental Dependent Arising: A Translation and Exposition of the Upanisa Sutta</h3><div class=\"bib\">Bhikkhu Bodhi<br>Buddhist Publication Society, Wheel 277/278; online edition © 1995.</div><p>A focused explanation of SN 12.23 and the conditions supporting liberation. Clearly represents a Theravāda exegetical approach.</p><p class=\"note\">Read after the basic twelve-link sequence to study the liberating path.</p><div class=\"locate\">Free authorized online text; a booklet, not a broad academic survey.</div><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/lib/authors/bodhi/wheel277.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read free · booklet ↗</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" data-category=\"research\" data-print-url=\"https://www.buddhistinquiry.org/article/dependent-arising/\"><div class=\"tag\">Scholarly article</div><h3>Dependent Arising</h3><div class=\"bib\">Bhikkhu Anālayo<br>Insight Journal / Barre Center for Buddhist Studies, 2020.</div><p>Distinguishes specific conditionality from particular lists of links. Particularly useful for understanding why shorter early formulations remain valid.</p><p class=\"note\">A concise conceptual starting point; historical proposals within it should be read as scholarly arguments.</p><div class=\"locate\">Free article; not a book.</div><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"https://www.buddhistinquiry.org/article/dependent-arising/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read free · article ↗</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" id=\"book-kalupahana\" data-category=\"advanced\"><div class=\"tag\">Philosophy · advanced</div><h3>Causality: The Central Philosophy of Buddhism</h3><div class=\"bib\">David J. Kalupahana<br>University Press of Hawaii, 1975.</div><p>Begin with Chapters V–VI; pp. 89–95 discuss conditionality and fatalism. Compares Pāli and Chinese sources.</p><p class=\"note\">An influential philosophical interpretation to compare with other accounts, not a consensus statement.</p><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"#study-arrows\" data-study=\"study-arrows\">Explore the study note ↑</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" id=\"book-gombrich\" data-category=\"advanced\"><div class=\"tag\">Historical interpretation</div><h3>How Buddhism Began: The Conditioned Genesis of the Early Teachings</h3><div class=\"bib\">Richard F. Gombrich<br>Routledge, 2nd edition, 2006; first edition 1996.</div><p>Read Chapter II, especially pp. 45–48, and the second-edition introduction. Examines conditioned consciousness and Brahmanical context.</p><p class=\"note\">Distinguish textual observations from proposals about the historical development of the teachings.</p><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"#study-history\" data-study=\"study-history\">Explore the study note ↑</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" id=\"book-garfield\" data-category=\"advanced\"><div class=\"tag\">Madhyamaka · translation & commentary</div><h3>The Fundamental Wisdom of the Middle Way: Nāgārjuna’s Mūlamadhyamakakārikā</h3><div class=\"bib\">Jay L. Garfield<br>Oxford University Press, 1995.</div><p>Begin with Chapter 24, especially verses 18–19, then Chapter 26 on the twelve links; read the accompanying commentary.</p><p class=\"note\">Garfield states an Indo-Tibetan Prāsaṅgika standpoint. This is a later philosophical treatment.</p><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"#study-emptiness\" data-study=\"study-emptiness\">Explore the study note ↑</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" id=\"book-jurewicz\" data-category=\"research\"><div class=\"tag\">Historical argument · article</div><h3>Playing with Fire: The pratītyasamutpāda from the Perspective of Vedic Thought</h3><div class=\"bib\">Joanna Jurewicz<br>Journal of the Pali Text Society 26 (2000), pp. 77–103.</div><p>A proposed reading of the twelve-link formula as a response to Vedic cosmogony. The supplied copy is a later reprint with different pagination.</p><p class=\"note\">A tentative historical reconstruction; does not cover every canonical formulation.</p><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"#study-history\" data-study=\"study-history\">Explore the study note ↑</a></div></article><article class=\"book\" id=\"book-choong\" data-category=\"advanced\"><div class=\"tag\">Comparative study · further reading</div><h3>The Fundamental Teachings of Early Buddhism</h3><div class=\"bib\">Choong Mun-keat<br>Harrassowitz, 2000.</div><p>A comparative study based on the Sūtrāṅga portion of the Pāli Saṃyutta-Nikāya and the Chinese Saṃyuktāgama. The “Causal condition” chapter is pp. 150–204.</p><p class=\"note\">Recommended for extended study. This chapter has not been checked for the present annotations; the available extract covers pp. 206–207.</p><div class=\"access\"><a href=\"https://www.buddhism.hku.hk/wp-content/uploads/BSTC2025-TBC.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chapter reference · HKU reading list ↗</a></div></article></div><p class=\"meta-scope\" style=\"margin-top:18px\">External links lead to publishers or authorized reading pages. Publisher pages may require browser verification or regional access. Book details for Gethin and Harvey were checked against the supplied editions. No uploaded book PDFs are redistributed here.</p>\n<div class=\"source-register\"><h3>Read the discourses directly</h3><p class=\"note\">English translations by Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu, hosted by Access to Insight. SN = Saṃyutta Nikāya; MN = Majjhima Nikāya; DN = Dīgha Nikāya.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.002.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SN 12.2 — Analysis of Dependent Co-arising</a>: the main formula and definitions.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.067.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SN 12.67 — Sheaves of Reeds</a>: reciprocal conditioning.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.023.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SN 12.23 — Prerequisites</a>: a sequence supporting liberation.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/sn/sn12/sn12.065.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SN 12.65 — The City</a>: inquiry into conditionality and the Noble Eightfold Path.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/mn/mn.148.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MN 148 — The Six Sextets</a>: sense experience, contact, feeling, and responses.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/dn/dn.15.0.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DN 15 — The Great Causes Discourse</a>: an extended inquiry, including reciprocal conditioning of consciousness and name-and-form.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/mn/mn.038.than.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MN 38 — The Greater Craving-Destruction Discourse</a>: consciousness arises through conditions; the discourse challenges the claim that the same consciousness continues through rebirth.</li></ul><p><a href=\"#study-parallels\" data-study=\"study-parallels\">Read the four Pāli–Chinese comparisons ↑</a></p></div>\n</section><footer class=\"site-footer\"><p>Beyond the Conditioned · Independent Buddhist study · Revised 18 September 2026<br>Based on the listed sources; not independently peer reviewed. No endorsement by cited authors or institutions is implied.</p><p>Texts and annotations are concise paraphrases. Other Buddhist traditions and scholars may explain particular links differently. This page uses no analytics, accounts, cookies, or trackers — a deliberate privacy choice, so study remains unmeasured. External reading links open other websites. Paper is the default study palette; a night palette is available from the header.</p><a href=\"#main\">Back to the model ↑</a></footer>\n</main>\n<dialog id=\"modal\" aria-labelledby=\"modal-title\"><div class=\"modalhead\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\" id=\"modal-crumb\" hidden></p><h2 id=\"modal-title\"></h2></div><button id=\"close-modal\" aria-label=\"Close dialog\">✕</button></div><div class=\"modalbody\" id=\"modal-body\"></div></dialog>\n<noscript><div class=\"noscript\">Enable JavaScript to explore this presentation. The standard sequence is: ignorance → volitional formations → consciousness → name-and-form → six sense bases → contact → feeling → craving → clinging → becoming → birth → aging-and-death. SN 12.2 also presents the cessation of this sequence.</div></noscript>";
